{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"cd /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-14_18-14-25-hashicorp_raft-real-run/draft && python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\ndefect=json.load(open('update32.json'))['interpretations'][0]   # current replay account (cites knowledge-2 rev 2)\ncorr={\n \\\"investigation_id\\\":\\\"29e4214f5ee146b393957fa23a33a73d-question-0\\\",\n \\\"source_ids\\\":[\\\"src_should_snapshot\\\",\\\"src_snapshot_cadence\\\",\\\"src_default_config\\\"],\n \\\"executions\\\":{\\\"0cb30b6bf8874d479b83653bd4e6c553\\\":[36,38,41]},\n \\\"conclusion\\\":\\\"explained\\\",\n \\\"defect_claim\\\":None,\n \\\"rationale\\\":(\\\"Correction account for the refined snapshot-cadence behaviour, needed because the earlier accounts of this question described the automatic cadence as keeping the committed range above the snapshot near SnapshotThreshold. \\\"\n  \\\"Source: shouldSnapshot compares the log store's last index with the last snapshot index and returns true at or above the threshold (snapshot.go:107-121), but runSnapshots only evaluates it when a random timeout of up to SnapshotInterval expires (snapshot.go:71-95), and the defaults are a threshold of 8192 with an interval of 120s (config.go:331-342). \\\"\n  \\\"The range can therefore pass the threshold and keep growing until the next check, which the retained default-configuration receipt shows from the other side: with the threshold at 8192 and no snapshot on the store, the node's committed range stood 12001 entries above the snapshot at restart, no snapshot had been taken, and the replay-enabled restart did not return while the flag-off control returned in 3ms (receipt 0cb30b6bf8874d479b83653bd4e6c553, events 36, 38 and 41; cited as non-supporting corroboration because this account makes no defect judgment). \\\"\n  \\\"Consequences for the affected accounts: statements that the cadence keeps the range near the threshold overstate how tight the automatic bound is, and the reachable replay above a snapshot is correspondingly larger between checks; the mechanism of the replay block, the 128-batch channel, the measured 128/129 boundary and the consequences recorded for this question are unaffected, and the successor account for the question cites knowledge revision 2.\\\"),\n \\\"answers\\\":{\n  \\\"d28b3b9b47b94197b7be7a32b20158f7-gap-5\\\":{\n    \\\"explanation\\\":\\\"The corrected premise is that the snapshot check is evaluated only on a randomized timeout of up to SnapshotInterval, so the committed range above the newest snapshot can exceed SnapshotThreshold by a wide margin: measured as 12001 replayed entries above an absent snapshot against a threshold of 8192, with no snapshot listed at restart (receipt 0cb30b6bf8874d479b83653bd4e6c553, event 36). Knowledge revision 2 states this.\\\",\n    \\\"resolved\\\":True},\n  \\\"d28b3b9b47b94197b7be7a32b20158f7-gap-6\\\":{\n    \\\"explanation\\\":\\\"Same basis as the companion answer: the correction changes how tight the automatic snapshot bound is, not the replay mechanism. The blocked restart in the corroborating receipt (event 38) and the returning control (event 41) follow the same path the question describes, with knowledge-2 revision 2 and knowledge-1 explaining why the replay is bounded by the committed range above the snapshot.\\\",\n    \\\"resolved\\\":True},\n  \\\"d28b3b9b47b94197b7be7a32b20158f7-gap-7\\\":{\n    \\\"explanation\\\":\\\"Same basis. For the older accounts that cited the earlier wording, the practical difference is the size of the range that can accumulate between checks: with the default interval of 120s a node committing faster than that cadence carries a range above the bound into its restart, which is what the default-configuration pair measures over two executions (receipts 0cb30b6bf8874d479b83653bd4e6c553 and 9039d268ffc24fa085efc75de7dc4fb2).\\\",\n    \\\"resolved\\\":True},\n  \\\"d28b3b9b47b94197b7be7a32b20158f7-gap-8\\\":{\n    \\\"explanation\\\":\\\"Same basis. The remaining uncertainty is not about the premise but about its range of application: the receipt is a burst workload, no steady-state measurement of how long a node typically stays above the threshold is offered, and the automatic-snapshot overshoot pattern (a snapshot completing while a range is already above the bound) is not exercised.\\\",\n    \\\"resolved\\\":True}},\n \\\"knowledge\\\":[{\\\"entry_id\\\":\\\"29e4214f5ee146b393957fa23a33a73d-knowledge-2\\\",\\\"revision\\\":2,\\\"assertion\\\":\\\"The snapshot check runs only on a randomized timeout of up to SnapshotInterval, so the committed range above the newest snapshot can exceed SnapshotThreshold by a wide margin between checks.\\\"}],\n \\\"counterevidence\\\":[\n  \\\"The margin measurement is one workload on one node; it shows that the default threshold and interval do not bound the range tightly, not how large the range becomes under a steady write pattern.\\\",\n  \\\"The corrected premise does not weaken any recorded conclusion: the block, the measured bound, the boundary pair and the availability consequence all rest on the number of delivered batches, which the cadence only affects through the size of the replayed range.\\\",\n  \\\"This account makes no defect judgment of its own; the experiment-cited account for the question remains the defect account and now cites knowledge revision 2.\\\"],\n \\\"limitations\\\":[\n  \\\"One execution of the default-configuration pair and its repeat are cited as corroboration; a steady-state measurement of the range above the threshold across snapshot cycles is not offered.\\\",\n  \\\"The earlier accounts keep their original wording in the retained history; only their recorded uses are marked as affected.\\\"]\n}\njson.dump({\\\"rationale\\\":\\\"Answers the four gaps opened by the snapshot-cadence correction with a source-only account that states the refined premise and its limits, and re-appends the experiment-cited defect account so the question keeps its defect judgment.\\\",\n \\\"interpretations\\\":[corr,defect]},open('update33.json','w'),indent=2)\nprint([ (i['conclusion'],i.get('defect_claim'),len(i.get('answers',{}))) for i in (corr,defect)])\nPY\ncd /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-14_18-14-25-hashicorp_raft-real-run && /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/.venv/bin/python -I -B -c \\\"import sys; sys.path.insert(0, '/home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/src'); from consensus_assurance.cli import main; raise SystemExit(main())\\\" validate --run /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-14_18-14-25-hashicorp_raft-real-run --submission update33.json 2>&1 | python3 -c \\\"\nimport sys,json; d=json.load(sys.stdin)\nprint('valid',d['valid'],'no_change',d.get('no_change'))\nfor x in d.get('diagnostics',[])[:8]: print(x['paths'],x['message'])\nprint([(e['object'],e['action']) for e in d.get('effects',[])])\n\\\"\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
